  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1133 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nTwo upper level low pressure systems will impact parts of central\nCONUS through the Thanksgiving Holiday, bringing the potential\nfor wintry precipitation to the Mid-South. The southernmost low\nwill track near the Gulf Coast on Wednesday, allowing a plume of\nmoisture to advect into the region. Simultaneously, the northern\nstream system will advance out of the northern Plains into the\nOzarks. At this point, guidance varies in respect to the\nprogression of the lows. The GFS is the outlier in this scenario\nas it treks the southern stream low into the Mid-Atlantic before\nits eventual phasing with the northernmost system. Both the ECMWF\nand Canadian depict the lows phasing much earlier, resulting in\ngreater wintry precipitation chances in the Mid-South. Surface\ntemperatures will hover around freezing Wednesday night, but one\nthing to watch will be the potential for dynamic cooling as\nhydrometeors make their way through the atmospheric column. If\ndynamic cooling is realized, heavy snow showers cannot be ruled\nout, particularly in northeast Arkansas and the Missouri\nBootheel. One specific area of interest will be locations along\nCrowley's Ridge as this setup may result in greater snow chances\nat slightly higher elevations.\n\nOne important thing to note: this event will be low probability,\nbut high impact. The reasonable worst case scenario would be\nheavy snow showers forming and dropping around an inch of snow.\nSome of the latest guidance is trending towards this scenario as\nthe latest Extreme Forecast Index (EFI) and Shift of Tails (SOT)\ndepict maximum EFI values and high SOT contours. This combination\nyields a higher confidence in an extreme event. Meaning, greater\npotential for snow showers. In addition, the latest REFS is\ndepicting snow band development well into west Tennessee. At this\ntime, confidence remains too low to include the aforementioned\nareas in a Winter Weather Advisory. However, if the reasonable\nworst case scenario does play out, a winter weather product may\nbe needed. For now, kept snowfall totals to a trace but this may\nneed to be updated as we move closer to the event.\n\nPrecipitation will wane as we move into Thursday night, where a\nfew stray flakes cannot be ruled out. Temperatures on Friday will\nbe chilly with highs reaching the upper 40s and lower 50s. On\nSaturday, upper level low pressure will finally pull away from\nthe Mid-South. However, both the GFS and ECMWF depict a shortwave\ndeveloping on the backside of this system. This will increase\nrain chances on Sunday. For now, rainfall totals look meager. An\nactive synoptic pattern will persist into the beginning of next\nweek. While too early to tell specifics, additional chances for\nprecipitation cannot be ruled out next week.",
